A conception of God that collapses under sincere questioning is probably protecting human authority, not divine truth.

Questioning can be reverence: taking truth seriously enough to test what people claim about it. In scripture itself, people argue, lament, doubt, wrestle, and demand answers. The fragile party is usually the interpreter, institution, or ego that has mistaken its map for God.

And miss-taken lands: the questioner is often not lost—merely taken wrongly by those who need obedience to resemble faith. Question boldly; just remain equally willing to question the answers you prefer.